<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">British Burma</h1><p class="st-meta">1824 CE – 1948 CE · capital Rangoon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Burma under British rule, built up through three wars from 1824 and governed as part of British India until 1937, then as a separate colony.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mrauk-u-kingdom">Arakan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a>, settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-yandabo">Treaty of Yandabo</a> in 1826. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-burmese-war">Second Anglo-Burmese War</a> in 1852 added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a>. In 1885 to 1886 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed the rest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty</a>'s kingdom after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-burmese-war">Third Anglo-Burmese War</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Burma</a> was governed as a province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-raj">British India</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> as its capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Irrawaddy</a> delta was cleared for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> the world's largest rice exporter, worked by Burman migrants and financed by Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chettiar">Chettiar</a> moneylenders. Teak, oil and rubies were exploited. Buddhist associations and students led nationalist protests, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saya-san">Saya San</a> rebellion of 1930 to 1932 was suppressed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Burma</a> was separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1937. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied it from 1942, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-burma">State of Burma</a>, but <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> returned in 1945. Negotiations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-peoples-freedom-league">Anti-Fascist People's Freedom League</a> led to independence on 4 January 1948 as the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-burma.jpg" alt="British Burma" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Beao (original) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Burma_(1939%E2%80%931941,_1945%E2%80%931948).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1824 CE – 1948 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rangoon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Reginald Dorman-Smith, Hubert Rance</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Burmese, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Myanmar</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Burma Province, Colony of Burma, Province of Burma</dd></dl></aside></div>
